          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would changing when I eat help?",
          "answer": "Changing meal timing can have a notable impact on health and well-being. Research indicates that both the size of meals and the timing at which they are consumed can influence symptoms such as heartburn. Larger meals can distend the stomach, increasing pressure against the esophagus, which may lead to reflux symptoms. Eating smaller meals can help mitigate this effect (source: lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n\nAdditionally, timing can matter greatly, especially when aligning meals with activities. For instance, leaving a gap of several hours between the last meal and going to bed can help prevent nocturnal heartburn. This is because lying down can exacerbate the effects of a full stomach (source: lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n\nOther conditions, such as diabetes, also emphasize the importance of meal timing, particularly for those on medications with fixed dosing schedules (source: lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet). \n\nIf you are considering changing when you eat, it may be helpful to discuss this with a healthcare provider, particularly to tailor any changes to your specific needs and symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
